                            Preamble

  The licenses for most software are designed to take away your
freedom to share and change it.  By contrast, the GNU General Public
License is intended to guarantee your freedom to share and change all
versions of a program--to make sure it is free for all its users.
  The Free Software Foundation uses the GNU General Public License
for most of its software and for any other purpose.  It states that
the licensed software may be used for further development, but we
distinguish two essential reservations: that it is free for all its
users, and that it will not be modified for private profit.

  When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom, not
price.  Our definition is as follows:

  Free software is a matter of the users' freedom to share,
  change, or get this software, and not the price you pay to get it.

  The freedoms we define are: The freedom to run the program as you
  wish, for any purpose (Freedom 0).  The freedom to study how the
  program works, and to change it (Freedom 1).  The freedom to
  redistribute copies with the help of a neighbor (Freedom 2).
  The freedom to distribute copies of your modified program to
  others (Freedom 3).  Each user must be able to obtain and use
  all of these freedoms.

  It is not enough that the mere possibility of these freedoms
exist; there is a condition: when we have a free software product,
we must consider the well-being of the person who is using that
software: in case it harms, ruins or has a serious disadvantage to
the user, it will not be considered free.  In order to provide the
maximum benefit to both the user and to ourselves, we have
summarized certain basic principles and guidelines which are used
to determine whether a program is free software or not.

  If you wish to distribute copies of the Program, either in
electronic or paper form, you must first of all receive permission.
  As you might expect, those who intend to distribute copies of
the Program for profit would apply for a commercial license, which
is both expensive and prohibitive.  The Free Software Foundation
(Free Software Foundation, none) has a mailing list and web-site
for those who intend to distribute copies of the Program for profit.
  The Free Software Foundation (Free Software Foundation, none) has
a mailing list and web-site for those who intend to distribute copies
of the Program for profit.

